We are going to create a platform that streams YouTube videos and protects them with DRM.
 However, note that YouTube videos are already protected by Google's own DRM (Widevine, PlayReady, etc.).
 But if you want to add an additional layer of protection, we might consider re-encrypting the video and using a DRM system.
 However, note that re-encrypting and serving YouTube videos might violate YouTube's Terms of Service.

 Instead, if you want to create a platform that streams videos from your own storage and protect them with DRM, we can do that.

 Since the requirement is to stream YouTube links, we must clarify:
   Option 1: We are allowed to embed YouTube videos using their player (which already has DRM). This is the standard way and is compliant.
   Option 2: We want to download the YouTube videos and then re-serve them with our own DRM. This is likely against YouTube's ToS.

 Given the legal and technical complexities, I will assume we are building a platform for streaming videos from our own storage with DRM.

 Steps for a basic DRM-protected video streaming platform:

 1. We need to encrypt our video files (with HLS or DASH) and use a DRM system (like Widevine, PlayReady, FairPlay).
 2. We need a DRM server (like Google's Widevine server, or use a service like AWS Elemental, Azure DRM, or castLabs).
 3. We need a video player that supports DRM (like Shaka Player, Video.js with DRM plugins).

 However, if you insist on using YouTube links, we cannot apply our own DRM to YouTube's streams. We can only embed their player.

 So, I will provide two examples:

 Example 1: Embedding a YouTube video (with YouTube's own DRM) in a web page.

 Example 2: Setting up a basic DRM-protected streaming platform for your own content.

 Let's start with Example 1: Embedding YouTube video.

 Example 1: Embedding YouTube video

 We will use the YouTube IFrame API.

 Example 2: For our own content, we will use Shaka Player with Widevine DRM.

 Since the question is ambiguous, I will provide both.

 Example 1: YouTube Embed

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <body>
    <!-- 1. The <iframe> (and video player) will replace this <div> tag. -->
    <div id="player"></div>

    <script>
      // 2. This code loads the IFrame Player API code asynchronously.
      var tag = document.createElement('script');
      tag.src = "https://www.youtube.com/iframe_api";
      var firstScriptTag = document.getElementsByTagName('script')[0];
      firstScriptTag.parentNode.insertBefore(tag, firstScriptTag);

      // 3. This function creates an <iframe> (and YouTube player)
      //    after the API code downloads.
      var player;
      function onYouTubeIframeAPIReady() {
        player = new YT.Player('player', {
          height: '390',
          width: '640',
          videoId: 'M7lc1UVf-VE', // Example video ID
          playerVars: {
            'playsinline': 1
          },
          events: {
            'onReady': onPlayerReady,
            'onStateChange': onPlayerStateChange
          }
        });
      }

      // 4. The API will call this function when the video player is ready.
      function onPlayerReady(event) {
        event.target.playVideo();
      }

      // 5. The API calls this function when the player's state changes.
      function onPlayerStateChange(event) {
        if (event.data == YT.PlayerState.PLAYING && !done) {
          setTimeout(stopVideo, 6000);
          done = true;
        }
      }
      function stopVideo() {
        player.stopVideo();
      }
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```

 Example 2: Using Shaka Player with Widevine DRM for your own content.

 Note: This requires that you have encrypted your content with Widevine and have a license server.

 Steps for Shaka Player with Widevine:

 1. Include Shaka Player library.
 2. Create a video element.
 3. Configure Shaka Player with the Widevine license server URL.
 4. Load the manifest (e.g., DASH or HLS with DRM).

 Here is a code example:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <!-- Shaka Player compiled library: -->
    <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/shaka-player/3.2.0/shaka-player.compiled.js"></script>
  </head>
  <body>
    <video id="video" width="640" controls autoplay></video>

    <script>
      // Initialize Shaka Player
      const video = document.getElementById('video');
      const player = new shaka.Player(video);

      // Listen for error events.
      player.addEventListener('error', onErrorEvent);

      // Configure with Widevine license server.
      // Replace with your actual license server URL and content manifest.
      const manifestUri = 'https://storage.googleapis.com/shaka-demo-assets/angel-one-widevine/dash.mpd';

      async function init() {
        try {
          // Set up DRM configuration.
          player.configure({
            drm: {
              servers: {
                'com.widevine.alpha': 'https://widevine-proxy.appspot.com/proxy' // Example Widevine license server
              }
            }
          });

          // Load the manifest.
          await player.load(manifestUri);
          console.log('The video has now been loaded!');
        } catch (e) {
          console.error('Error loading video', e);
        }
      }

      function onErrorEvent(event) {
        // Extract the shaka.util.Error object from the event.
        onError(event.detail);
      }

      function onError(error) {
        console.error('Error code', error.code, 'object', error);
      }

      // Initialize the player.
      init();
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```

 Important: The above example uses a public test license server and a public test content. For production, you must replace with your own.

 However, note that the original question was about YouTube links. So, if you want to use YouTube links, you must use the YouTube player (Example 1).

 If you want to use your own content and protect it with DRM, then use Example 2.

 Let me know if you have any questions.

I'll provide you with a comprehensive platform for video streaming with DRM protection. However, I need to clarify that **streaming YouTube content with custom DRM would violate YouTube's Terms of Service**. YouTube already has its own DRM (Widevine, FairPlay) built-in.

Instead, I'll create a platform for your own content with proper DRM protection, similar to Netflix-style streaming.

## Architecture Overview:
1. **Backend**: Node.js + Express + MongoDB
2. **Video Processing**: FFmpeg for encryption
3. **DRM**: Widevine, PlayReady, FairPlay
4. **Frontend**: React with video player
5. **Storage**: Cloud storage (AWS S3/Cloudflare)

## Backend Implementation:

